# Brazilian Soccer MCP Server

An **MCP (Model Context Protocol) server** that exposes Brazilian soccer data —
matches, teams, players and competitions — from the provided Kaggle datasets so
that an LLM client can answer natural‑language questions about Brazilian
football.

The specification lives in [`brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md`](brazilian-soccer-mcp-guide.md)
(`TASK.md` is the task brief).

## What was built

* A normalised, in‑memory **data layer** that loads all six provided CSV files
  and unifies their different schemas (state suffixes, accented vs ascii names,
  three different date formats, missing/"not played" goals).
* A **team‑name normalizer** that collapses the many spelling variants of each
  club (`Flamengo`, `Flamengo-RJ`, `Flamengo - RJ`) to a single canonical key so
  the same team can be matched across files.
* A **query engine** implementing every capability category required by the
  spec: match queries, team queries, player queries, competition queries and
  statistical analysis.
* An **MCP server** (`mcp` v2 / `MCPServer`) exposing 16 tools, one per
  capability, each returning JSON that an LLM can consume.
* A **BDD (Gherkin + pytest‑bdd) test suite** plus integration, performance and
  sample‑question coverage tests.

## Project layout

```
.
├── data/kaggle/                       # the six provided datasets
├── src/brazilian_soccer_mcp/
│   ├── __init__.py
│   ├── normalizer.py                   # team-name canonicalization + derbies
│   ├── data.py                         # loads & normalizes all CSVs
│   ├── queries.py                      # QueryEngine (the query API)
│   └── server.py                       # MCP server (tools) + stdio entrypoint
├── tests/
│   ├── conftest.py
│   ├── features/                       # Gherkin .feature files (BDD scenarios)
│   ├── test_match_queries.py
│   ├── test_team_queries.py
│   ├── test_player_queries.py
│   ├── test_competition_queries.py
│   ├── test_statistical_queries.py
│   ├── test_data_coverage.py
│   ├── test_server.py                  # MCP tool registry + stdio end-to-end
│   ├── test_performance.py             # <2s lookups, <5s aggregates
│   └── test_sample_questions.py        # 20+ spec sample questions
└── pyproject.toml
```

## Install

```bash
pip install -e .[test]
```

Dependencies: `pandas`, `numpy`, `mcp>=2`. Test extras: `pytest`, `pytest-bdd`.

## Run the tests

```bash
python -m pytest
```

20 BDD scenarios (Gherkin `Given/When/Then`) plus integration, performance and
sample‑question tests — 51 tests total, all passing.

## Run the MCP server

Stdio transport (for an MCP‑aware LLM client):

```bash
python -m brazilian_soccer_mcp
```

Or the installed console script:

```bash
brazilian-soccer-mcp
```

A client connects over stdio, calls `initialize`, `tools/list` and
`tools/call` exactly as in `tests/test_server.py::test_mcp_stdio_end_to_end`.

## Available MCP tools

| Tool | Description |
|------|-------------|
| `find_matches` | Matches by team, opponent, competition, season and/or date range |
| `head_to_head` | Head‑to‑head record between two teams |
| `last_match_between` | Most recent match played between two teams |
| `team_statistics` | Win/draw/loss & goal stats for a team (by season/venue) |
| `team_competitions` | Competitions a team has appeared in |
| `search_players` | FIFA players by name/nationality/club/position/rating |
| `top_brazilian_players` | Top‑rated Brazilian players |
| `players_at_club` | All FIFA players at a club |
| `brazilian_players_by_club` | Brazilian players per Brazilian club with avg rating |
| `standings` | League standings calculated from match results (3 pts/win) |
| `biggest_wins` | Biggest victories by goal margin |
| `average_goals` | Average goals/match and home/away win/draw rates |
| `best_home_record` | Best home win rates |
| `best_away_record` | Best away win rates |
| `derbies` | Traditional Brazilian derby matches |
| `seasons_summary` | Per‑season aggregate summary |

